WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.400
Hi Steve Kaufmann here today, and
I want to talk about why you do not

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.750
need to be perfect in any foreign
language that you're learning.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.910
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, uh, click

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.470
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.490
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.880
So why do I want to talk about perfection?

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.315
First of all, I am a dilettante learner.

00:00:27.345 --> 00:00:31.875
I also recognize that there are people
who need the language for professional

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:37.785
reasons because they want to get into a
school so that they're not just dabbling

00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:42.435
in the language, exploring, having fun
like I like to do, they actually need it.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:46.725
So how good do they need
to be in the language?

00:00:48.495 --> 00:00:52.545
First of all, there is an expression
many of you have heard and an expression

00:00:52.545 --> 00:00:57.584
that exists in many languages uh, the
perfect can be the enemy of the good.

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:02.474
So if you try to be perfect, you
may end up not being very good.

00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:07.695
Uh, if you are too obsessed with being
perfect, you might not communicate

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:13.335
as well as someone else who is simply
trying to communicate without worrying

00:01:13.335 --> 00:01:15.375
about being perfect, because...

00:01:16.265 --> 00:01:20.505
you know, speaking a foreign language
is not like being in the diving

00:01:20.505 --> 00:01:22.695
competition at the Olympic Games.

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:26.985
Uh, it's not like designing a
rocket that's going to go to

00:01:27.015 --> 00:01:29.385
the moon or building a bridge.

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:31.575
Uh, there, we have to be perfect.

00:01:31.605 --> 00:01:35.295
Even if you're building a simple
structure, you've got to have 90 degree

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.035
angles, things have to be straight.

00:01:37.275 --> 00:01:38.865
There's no room for sloppiness.

00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:43.985
However, in communicating in the language,
there's lots of room for sloppiness.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.020
Uh, you can still communicate.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.100
You can still understand.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.530
So how good do we need to be?

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:54.780
First of all, in my experience, as
a person who has worked with people

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.010
from many different countries where
sometimes we speak English and sometimes

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:05.670
we speak the other person's native
language, rarely, rarely have I dealt

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.370
with people who spoke English perfectly.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.009
It didn't matter whether
they were Swedish, German,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.560
French, Spanish, Japanese.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.070
If they were speaking English,
there was always something

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.130
there with which suggested that
they weren't native speakers.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.120
Uh, similarly, when I speak
those languages, they know

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.770
that I'm not a native speaker.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.430
However, we aren comfortable
communicating with each other.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:35.700
I do think that, uh, the standard
required for written communication

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.650
is usually higher than the standard
required for oral communication.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.260
Uh, use of words, uh,
some aspects of grammar.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:51.555
I think we're more willing to forgive
in spoken, uh, you know, conversations

00:02:51.815 --> 00:02:55.185
than we are if you get a formal letter,
you know, an extreme example would

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:59.955
be some legal letter or whatever,
but even a contract, uh, there, I

00:02:59.955 --> 00:03:05.175
think we have to be a little more
careful and the fact that we sort of

00:03:05.205 --> 00:03:07.245
give up on the idea of being perfect.

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:12.105
In other words, speaking with no
accent, making no mistakes, we recognize

00:03:12.105 --> 00:03:15.855
that we're not going to achieve
that, but that doesn't prevent us

00:03:15.855 --> 00:03:18.195
from wanting to continue to improve.

00:03:18.674 --> 00:03:21.195
So the question is, you know,
how do we continue to improve?

00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:27.945
So, uh, and bear in mind that people
who have, you know, I think of Henry

00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:32.115
Kissinger who we, you know, you can
still hear his German accent after,

00:03:32.115 --> 00:03:35.805
gosh, I don't know how many years
of working at the highest level of

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.475
diplomacy in the English language.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:43.185
So once we recognize that there are limits
to how good we can become, I think the

00:03:43.185 --> 00:03:45.255
question then is how do we become better?

00:03:45.285 --> 00:03:47.415
Because we would all like to be better.

00:03:47.565 --> 00:03:49.785
We would all like to continue to improve.

00:03:50.565 --> 00:03:56.015
And there, I think the, the, uh,
the issue is we have to be so

00:03:56.015 --> 00:04:00.335
comfortable, so natural in the
language that without thinking about

00:04:00.335 --> 00:04:04.380
it, we start to speak more correctly.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.100
We start to use words and very
often it's more a matter of use

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.770
of words then nitty points, nitty
biddy, nitpicking points of grammar.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.220
And I mean, you won't be surprised
to hear me say that that just

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.920
requires an awful lot of reading and
listening and particularly reading.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.870
If we want to write well, let's say we
have a job for a company that's uh...

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.355
deals in English and we're writing a,
you know, commercial correspondence

00:04:32.775 --> 00:04:39.675
you have to read a lot of that kind of
material, so that you end up gradually,

00:04:39.705 --> 00:04:46.485
naturally using words and using phrases
and structures and terminology, the way

00:04:46.545 --> 00:04:50.075
very proficient users of that language do.

00:04:50.685 --> 00:04:55.590
Uh, I think it can help at LingQ to
save key phrases that are very natural,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:05:00.300
very powerful that other people have
used so that we're imitating these very

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.159
good models of how to use the language.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:08.520
And I think if we want to get closer to,
if not perfection, a very high standard

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:13.545
in the language, I think it's important to
focus on our ability to write the language

00:05:13.635 --> 00:05:16.215
accurately, to use words accurately.

00:05:16.455 --> 00:05:20.055
And when you are writing, you have time
to think about what you want to say.

00:05:20.055 --> 00:05:24.315
You have time to look words up, you
have time to look even grammar rules up

00:05:24.435 --> 00:05:30.015
and gradually develop, you know, habits
that will carry over into your speaking.

00:05:30.525 --> 00:05:35.115
And so the sort of emphasis should
be on, you know, use of the language,

00:05:35.415 --> 00:05:37.905
not necessarily on how you pronounce.

00:05:38.429 --> 00:05:42.700
There's nothing wrong with wanting
to work on improving or making the

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.080
pronunciation more native-like,
but it's not as important.

00:05:46.469 --> 00:05:50.130
And I think we've all had the
experience of dealing with foreigners,

00:05:50.159 --> 00:05:53.940
you know, as an English speaker,
foreigners who use English very,

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.559
very well, but have an accent.

00:05:55.739 --> 00:05:59.520
And we're almost more impressed with
those people because they have an

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.650
accent yet they use our language so
elegantly, so accurately, so powerfully.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.740
And if we're able to do that
in a foreign language, even

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.110
with an accent, that's fine.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.460
I wanted to talk about people who
have a need to perform in the language

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.010
and what is a realistic expectation.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:22.030
And I don't think that
perfection is a realistic expert.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:27.099
But constant improvement, the
willingness to constantly improve and

00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:31.510
the desire to constantly engage with
the language, those are the things

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:33.190
that, that will keep us moving forward.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:34.320
I look forward  to hearing rom you.

